# The pinned upstreams, one per line: name url commit kind
#
# Read by tools/verify.py. Each one is also registered as a submodule, so the
# commit here is what the parent repository records as the gitlink and the two
# must agree - verify.py checks that, because a submodule advanced by a
# `git submodule update --remote` is a different measurement under the same
# name and nothing else would notice.
#
# Nothing here is vendored and nothing here is patched. Everything that
# measures or scores lives behind one of these three lines; this repository
# writes the Makefiles, the three stage tools, the docs and the data, and
# nothing else.
#
# Why each one is what it is:
#
#   flashwhisperer  the analysis half: the scoring rule, the row geometry, the
#                   AES reference. Every recovery in tools/reproduce.py goes
#                   through flashattack.archive.ladder at this commit.
#
#                   NOTE, because it changes what stage 3 can do. This commit's
#                   `archive.load` takes `(path, observable)` and REQUIRES a
#                   ciphertext column. Three capture classes here have no
#                   ciphertext column - a seed-only capture, a capture whose
#                   key or seed was a constant in the capture script, and every
#                   reduced file in traces/, whose ciphertexts were replaced by
#                   a SHA-256 on purpose - so tools/reproduce.py resolves those
#                   columns itself and hands the result to the pinned
#                   `archive.ladder`. The SCORER is upstream's at this commit;
#                   only the loading is local, and it collapses to one
#                   `archive.load` call when a loader that takes `key=`/`seed=`
#                   and reads a JSON capture is published.
#
#   crypto-eval     the instrument stages 1 and 2 run on: one part, one
#                   library, the victim's own cycle counter, the accelerator
#                   word read back off the device. At this commit it builds six
#                   ST parts; `python tools/build.py --list` prints which, and
#                   which of the roster's parts therefore cannot be rebuilt
#                   here yet.
#
#   demos           section 7 of the paper, the delivered attacks over real
#                   protocol stacks, each with the vectors it was measured
#                   from. This artifact covers sections 3 to 6 and does not
#                   duplicate any of it: its own README points here.
#
